13:10:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target: No. At most, international assessments can show differences in **average student performance on particular tests** at a particular time. They do not prove that a nationality or ethnic group is inherently less intelligent. For example, Ecuador’s results on international assessments have often been below OECD averages, but those scores are strongly affected by conditions such as: - school funding and teacher availability - poverty, nutrition, and childhood health - rural access to schools and internet - interruption of schooling or child labor - test language and familiarity with standardized-test formats - inequality between private and public schools - which students are enrolled and sampled Those are education and social-policy issues, not evidence of an inborn national trait. Countries’ scores can also change substantially over a generation when schooling and living conditions improve—something that would not make sense if the scores measured fixed intelligence. So a fair statement would be: “Ecuador has faced challenges in educational outcomes compared with wealthier countries.” It would not be fair or scientifically justified to turn that into “Ecuadorians aren’t sharp,” especially when judging individual coworkers.
