13:11:10 | INFO | 🤖 Target: They mean something real, but not what you are claiming. International math and science tests can measure how well sampled students perform on the tested material under standardized conditions. That is useful information about educational outcomes and, to some extent, learned skills. But a country’s average score is not a measure of every individual’s ability, nor proof of an innate trait of its people. The distinction is straightforward: - **Test result:** “In this sample and year, students in Country A answered fewer questions correctly on this assessment than students in Country B.” - **Possible interpretation:** differences in instruction, resources, attendance, health, language, curriculum, inequality, and test familiarity may contribute. - **Unsupported leap:** “People from Country A are inherently less intelligent.” A thermometer can give a real reading without telling you the cause of a fever. Likewise, test scores can be meaningful without supporting a stereotype about Ecuadorians.
