13:12:29 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is not crazy to notice that countries can produce different average outcomes in sports, school tests, or industries. The problem is treating those outcomes as evidence of an inborn mental ranking of nationalities. Sports are a useful example of why that inference fails. A country’s success can reflect participation rates, coaching, facilities, talent pipelines, funding, local popularity, scouting, and selection. A country that prioritizes basketball will produce more highly trained basketball players; that does not mean every person from there is naturally better at basketball. The same applies more strongly to education and cognitive-test outcomes. A country’s average reflects its schools, health and nutrition, inequality, language, migration patterns, opportunities, and who was tested. It cannot tell you that an individual Ecuadorian—or Ecuadorians as a people—has a fixed lower “brain” ability. So the sensible comparison is: use evidence relevant to the task. For basketball, watch someone play. For a job, assess their job skills. Nationality is a poor substitute for either.
