13:12:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target: People who study statistics, education, and workplace performance are not dismissing practical experience—they are warning against overgeneralizing from it. You can reasonably say: “On this site, some of the Ecuadorian workers I worked with needed more help with certain tasks.” That is a workplace observation. But it does not follow that Ecuadorians generally are less intelligent. Day-to-day experience is shaped by who gets hired into that crew, their prior training, language barriers, immigration and economic circumstances, and how tasks are taught. A different set of Ecuadorian workers—engineers, electricians, nurses, business owners, or experienced tradespeople—could give you a very different impression. The practical, non-academic solution is simple: use the same job-relevant standards for everyone—skills demonstrations, clear instructions, safety checks, quality control, and performance reviews. That protects both the company and workers better than assuming ability from nationality.
