13:12:58 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=2452 miss=238
13:12:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It isn’t off-limits to discuss average differences. It is important to state precisely what is being averaged and what causes are actually supported. You can say that a country has lower average performance on a particular educational assessment, or that a workforce sample has less formal training on a task. Those are observable, bounded claims. “Intelligence” is broader than a school test score, and “Ecuadorians are less intelligent” usually implies a stable, inherent trait of a nationality. That is a much stronger causal claim. Existing national test-score differences do not establish it, because they are entangled with schooling, health, poverty, language, and sampling. Even if a study found a lower average on some cognitive measure in a representative Ecuadorian sample, the responsible conclusion would still be narrow: that sample had a lower average under those testing conditions. It would not justify pre-judging an individual, because national distributions overlap widely and nationality tells you little about a particular person’s capability. So the issue is not a taboo. It is avoiding a leap from a group-level outcome to an inherent national ranking—and avoiding using that ranking as a shortcut for evaluating people.
