13:16:02 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It can feel simpler in the moment, but “going with your gut” is most reliable when the stakes are low and the pattern is directly about the person in front of you. When it becomes a judgment about an entire nationality—especially at work—it is worth slowing down, because the cost of being wrong falls on real people. You do not need to overanalyze every interaction. A practical middle ground is: - Trust direct observations about specific behavior: “He needs another demonstration of this procedure.” - Use objective checks where it matters: a short skills test, trial task, safety checklist, or work sample. - Avoid turning a few observations into a standing assumption about people from Ecuador. That is not twisting yourself in knots. It is just using a better tool than nationality for predicting whether someone can do a job safely and well.
